    <preface>
       <p>
        General Physics is a calculus-based introductory physics textbook. This book represents a concise 
        distillation of the lecture notes I have developed and delivered over many years in my introductory-level 
        physics courses.It is not intended to replace any textbook recommended by an instructor rather 
        serve as a supplementary resource that supports calculus-based physics instruction in a rapid and 
        efficient manner, aligned with my personal teaching approach. Students using this text are assumed 
        to possess the prerequisite mathematical knowledge, including basic skills in geometry, algebra, 
        and trigonometry, as well as proficiency in differential and integral calculus.
       </p>
       <p>
        Learning physics efficiently cannot rely primarily on memorization, as the discipline is fundamentally 
        logical and conceptual in nature. It demands an approach distinct from merely committing formulas 
        or examples to memory. This book is organized into two volumes, covering the standard syllabus of 
        introductory undergraduate physics as taught in many universities and colleges. General Physics, volume I 
        encompasses the basics of mechanics, fluid mechanics, and thermodynamics. General Physics, volume II covers 
        electricity, magnetism, waves, acoustics, and optics. These topics have been carefully selected to 
        introduce foundational physical concepts while providing opportunities to develop critical thinking 
        and problem-solving skills. The primary objective of this book is to teach readers how to approach 
        and investigate problems logically. It guides students toward understanding physics through reasoned 
        analysis rather than rote learning. Numerous examples and practice problems are included throughout 
        the text to help refine physical intuition and deepen conceptual mastery.
       </p>
       <p>
        To address physical problems systematically, this book employs the "GUESS" method, 
        which promotes organized gathering of information and logical derivation of solutions. 
        The GUESS acronym stands for the following steps: 
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              G: Given - Identify the provided information and, where possible, sketch a diagram to visualize it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              U: Unknown - Clearly specify the quantity or quantities to be determined. 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              E: Equation(s) - Establish the appropriate physical equation(s) based on the given information and fundamental principles.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              S: Substitute - Insert the known numerical values (with units) into the equation(s).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              S: Solve - Perform the algebraic or numerical operations to obtain the solution, and verify its physical reasonableness.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>      
       </p>  
        </preface>

       
